    {
      "id": "reading-pronunciation-entries",
      "title": "Reading dictionary pronunciation entries",
      "aka": ["dictionary transcription", "stress marks"],
      "tags": ["sounds", "dictionary", "transcription", "stress"],
      "summary": "How to read the IPA, stress marks and syllable breaks in a learner’s dictionary, in UK and US entries.",
      "levels": {"starter":["A learner’s dictionary tells you how to say a word. It shows the sounds in IPA between slashes, for example /əˈbaʊt/ for *about*. Many online dictionaries show a UK pronunciation and a US pronunciation, and an audio button for each.","The most useful symbol to learn first is the stress mark ˈ. It goes before the syllable that is strongest. In /əˈbaʊt/ the mark comes before /baʊt/, so the word is said a-BOUT.","To read an entry: find the stress mark, split the word into syllables, say each symbol, and then press the audio to check. A dot between letters, as in /ˈtiː.tʃə/, shows a syllable break.","Do not worry if the UK and US entries are different. They are two accents. Both are correct."],"builder":["Different dictionaries write the same sounds with slightly different symbols. A US entry may use /iː/, /ɑː/ and /e/ where this course writes /i/, /ɑ/ and /ɛ/. It may write /ɜːr/ and /ər/ where this course writes /ɝ/ and /ɚ/. A UK entry may write (r), as in /ˈtiːtʃə(r)/, for an /r/ that is pronounced only before a vowel. Read the key if a symbol is new.","Some words have two accepted pronunciations in one accent, such as GA /ˈiðɚ/ and /ˈaɪðɚ/ for *either*. They are separated by “or”. Neither one is wrong.","Part of speech matters. *Record* is /ˈrɛkɚd/ as a noun and /rɪˈkɔrd/ as a verb in GA. Always check that you are reading the entry for the correct part of speech.",{"table":[["Word","GA","SSBE"],["schedule","/ˈskɛdʒul/","/ˈʃedjuːl/"],["student","/ˈstudənt/","/ˈstjuːdənt/"],["tomato","/təˈmeɪtoʊ/","/təˈmɑːtəʊ/"]]},"When you check a word, use the same steps each time: find the stress mark, count the syllables, read the symbols, and press the audio. If you use a UK and a US entry together, check what differs. The most common differences are the *r* after a vowel, the vowel in words like *bath* and *hot*, and the /j/ in words like *new* and *student*.","Finally, do not worry about the small symbols at first. Your first goal is the stress and the main vowels. The rest comes with practice and with listening."],"pro":["A dictionary pronunciation is a citation form: a careful pronunciation of the word said alone. It does not show weak forms, linking, or assimilation. For function words, good dictionaries give both a strong and a weak form, such as *can* /kæn/ and /kən/. Speakers also vary within one accent, so a dictionary chooses the most widely accepted form or forms.","Conventions differ. Cambridge and Oxford learner’s dictionaries use IPA and show British and American pronunciation. Cambridge uses a dot to mark syllables and a small t̬ for the American flap. Oxford writes (r) for a linking /r/ in British entries. Merriam-Webster’s main entries use their own respelling system, not the IPA, so a reader who knows the IPA must learn its key.","The labels “UK” and “US” are approximations. The UK entries in learner’s dictionaries describe a standard southern British pronunciation, which is close to SSBE. The US entries describe a general American pronunciation. Neither covers all regional accents of its country.","A skilled reader uses a transcription as a hypothesis. They read it, say it, and check it against audio. When a learner’s own pronunciation differs from the dictionary, the question is whether the difference is a natural variant, an accent feature, or an error that may reduce understanding. Teachers who can explain the symbols can help learners answer it."]},
      "related": ["international-phonetic-alphabet", "schwa-sound", "english-vowels"],
      "read": [{"book":"eling","where":"The chapters on phonetics and phonology, in the sections on transcription, syllables and stress"}],
      "sources": [{"t":"Cambridge Dictionary: phonetic symbols and the IPA","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/help/phonetics.html"}, {"t":"Essentials of Linguistics, 2nd edition (eCampusOntario), free web book","url":"https://ecampusontario.pressbooks.pub/essentialsoflinguistics2/"}],
      "checked": "2026-11"
    }
